3. JWT authentication – fundamentals
JWT stands for JSON Web Token.
Imagine that a user registers on your AI platform and then logs in.
1.	User submits email and password.
2.	Backend verifies the password against its stored hash.
3.	Backend generates a signed JWT.
4.	Client stores the token securely.
5.	Client sends the token with subsequent API requests.
6.	Backend verifies the token and identifies the user.
A typical request header looks like this:
</>HTTP    
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN

A JWT has three sections:
</>Plain text
xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz
•	Header: token type and signing algorithm.
•	Payload: claims such as user ID and expiration.
•	Signature: verifies that the token has not been tampered with.
Security rule: JWT payloads are encoded, not encrypted. Never put passwords, API keys or other secrets inside them.
